[{:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://metaredux.com/posts/2026/11/01/nrepl-right-in-your-browser", :title "nREPL, Right in Your Browser", :link "https://metaredux.com/posts/2026/11/01/nrepl-right-in-your-browser.html", :published-at #inst "2026-11-01T09:06:00.000-00:00", :content "Last week nrepl.org got a makeover. The docs moved to docs.nrepl.org, and nrepl.org itself became a landing page that explains what nREPL is and how its protocol works, with “wire windows” showing real exchanges between a client and a server, as EDN or as the bencode that actually goes over the wire. Reading about a protocol only gets you so far, though, so the page now also has a “Try it” section with a real nREPL server running right in your browser. There’s one in this post as well! In the rest of it I’ll tell you how it came about, why it was harder than it looks, and how you can use the server behind it for your own projects. Here it is: user=> (+ 1 2) (println \"Hello from nREPL!\") (map inc (range 10)) (doc map) (range) interrupt show the messages Go ahead, evaluate something! Tick “show the messages” to see the requests and the responses going back and forth, and try (range) if you’re curious what happens to code that never finishes. Picking an approach An interactive nREPL playground always runs into the same problem: you need an nREPL server somewhere, and a server that evaluates code from random people on the internet is just about the worst thing you can host. (It would also cost money that I’d rather spend on something else.) So the server had to run in the browser, and I looked at a few ways to get there. The first one was the real thing. CheerpJ is a JVM that runs in the browser, and nREPL 1.8 and Clojure 1.12 worked on it remarkably well. Every op I tried worked, stdin included! The problem was everything around that - about 8 seconds to a ready server on a fast machine, about 4.5MB to download, a busy loop that freezes the whole page unless you put the JVM in a Web Worker, and an interrupt that works only on code that’s sleeping.1 That’s a lot to ask of someone who just opened a landing page. The second one was scittle, which runs SCI (Michiel Borkent’s Clojure interpreter) in the browser and comes with an nREPL server, sci.nrepl. It solves a different problem, though - it lets your editor evaluate code in a page, through a relay that runs in babashka or on the JVM, and its replies differ from nREPL’s in quite a few places. (I’ve filed some issues about those.) nbb has a pretty complete nREPL server of its own, but it runs only on Node. None of those fit, so I ended up writing a small nREPL server for SCI that runs entirely in a web page, with the page as its client - nrepl-sci. SCI does all the heavy lifting, and evaluation, completion and docs lookup took just a few hundred lines of ClojureScript. The whole thing is about 230KB gzipped and it’s ready in a fraction of a second. Not bad, right? The hard part A basic nREPL server is easy enough. The trouble starts with two ops most people never think about - interrupt and stdin. JavaScript gives you one thread, and SCI evaluates code in one go. While (loop [] (recur)) runs nothing else does, so the server can’t even read the interrupt request that would stop it, and code calling read-line can’t wait for input without blocking the very thread that would receive it. There’s a third problem hiding in there as well. nREPL sessions are supposed to be independent, so an eval in one session (say, your editor’s) shouldn’t hold up another one (say, a test runner connected to the same server). Here’s how nrepl-sci deals with all of that: The server runs in two Web Workers. A supervisor takes the requests, queues them, and answers interrupt and stdin itself, while an evaluator runs SCI. The two share some memory (a SharedArrayBuffer) that serves as a mailbox. The evaluator waits for mail with Atomics.wait, which blocks a worker without spinning, so read-line waits for its input there, much like it would on the JVM. SCI calls a hook of your choice on every function call and every recur. nrepl-sci uses it to check its mailbox, to see if it’s been interrupted, or if there’s a request from another session it could take in between. Checking on every call made code noticeably slower, so it checks on every 256th call, which still means every few microseconds. Benchmarks run about as fast as without any of this. Endless seqs like (range) and (iterate inc 0) check as they go, so (count (range)) stops when you interrupt it. Some code never calls back into SCI - a regex that backtracks forever, for instance. The supervisor gives it a second to stop, then kills the evaluator and starts a new one. Your sessions survive that, but everything you evaluated in them is gone. In the end the supervisor, the evaluator and the mailbox took more code than the nREPL server itself, which tells you something about where the complexity is. Note: All of this works only when the page is cross-origin isolated, as browsers hand out SharedArrayBuffer only to pages served with the Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy and Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy headers. Sadly, GitHub Pages, which hosts both nrepl.org and this blog, can’t send those. Without them nrepl-sci falls back to doing one thing at a time: read-line throws, and interrupting code means starting over, which is exactly what you saw if you tried (range) above. Evaluation, output, errors, completion and doc work the same either way, though. Testing with proof Back in 2023 I listed the lack of a protocol compatibility test suite among the things people hate about nREPL, and asked if one of you was looking for a fun small OSS project. Eventually I took my own advice and started proof, and nrepl-sci is the first server I’ve written with proof around from day one. It made a huge difference! Building an nREPL server used to involve a lot of guessing, as the protocol is small, but what clients actually expect from a server is spread across their source code. A proof profile is a short TOML file that tells proof how to start your server and how to say a few things in its language: a value, some output, an exception, reading a line, code that runs until it’s stopped. proof run takes it from there, with checks covering the protocol and the exact requests CIDER, Calva, Conjure, vim-fireplace, REPLy and neorepl send. When a check fails, proof also tells you why it matters: FAIL   robust.sessions-at-once      An eval in one session doesn't hold up the others\n       clone request got no reply with \"done\" within 10s\n       why: Each client has sessions of its own (e.g. an editor and neorepl,\n       connected to the same server), so a server that runs one eval at a time\n       leaves every other client waiting for whatever the code in one of them\n       does.\n That’s a check you can easily shrug off when you’re writing a server for a playground, and it’s the reason nrepl-sci takes requests in between at all. Working on the server mostly came down to running make proof after every change. Today nrepl-sci passes all 73 checks that apply to it (the other 5 are about things like vim-fireplace, which needs Java). Without shared memory it fails exactly two: stdin.need-input and robust.sessions-at-once. I guess that’s a pretty good summary of what you give up without cross-origin isolation. If you’re working on an nREPL server of your own, I can’t recommend proof enough. It also comes with a GitHub Action, so your CI can run it as well. The tradeoffs Nothing comes for free, and nrepl-sci is no exception: It’s SCI, not Clojure. You’re getting ClojureScript as interpreted by SCI, so there are no Java classes, no JVM libraries, and some corners of the language behave differently. That’s fine for a playground, but not for anything that needs your real project. There’s no JavaScript interop. SCI blocks it unless you allow it, and nrepl-sci doesn’t, so (js/alert \"hi\") and even (.toUpperCase \"hi\") fail. Admittedly, that’s a bit annoying, but it’s also what makes nrepl-sci safe to embed anywhere, as the code people type can’t touch the page, or even the worker it runs in. (clojure.string/upper-case works just fine.) A request from another session runs on top of the code it interrupted, so that code can’t stop until the request is done, and a read-line taken in between holds it until it gets its input. You’d need two clients fighting over the same server to notice. Starting over loses your definitions, which matters most without cross-origin isolation, where every interrupt starts over. It’s about 1MB of JavaScript (230KB gzipped), loaded the first time you use the REPL. That’s nothing compared to CheerpJ, but it’s not free either. Using nrepl-sci yourself nrepl-sci was built for nrepl.org, but nothing in it is specific to the site. You can embed a REPL in a page - documentation, a tutorial, a blog post. The worker takes the bencode of each request as a Uint8Array and posts back the bencode of each reply, so the whole integration boils down to something like this: const worker = new Worker(\"/assets/js/nrepl-sci-worker-0.1.0.js\");\nworker.onmessage = (event) => console.log(decode(event.data));\nworker.postMessage(new TextEncoder().encode(encode({ op: \"eval\", code: \"(+ 1 2)\", id: \"1\" })));\n encode and decode are a few dozen lines of bencode, and the REPL in this post is about 170 lines in total, styles included - have a look. Grab nrepl-sci-worker.js from the latest release and serve it from your own site, as browsers start workers only from the page’s own origin. If you can send the cross-origin isolation headers, you get stdin and proper interrupts for free. nrepl.org’s playground is a bigger example, with completion, history and the wire windows. You can also test nREPL clients without a JVM. The Node build is a regular nREPL server on a socket (node nrepl-sci.js --port 7888) that’s up in about a tenth of a second, which makes it handy for the test suites of clients and editor plugins. Finally, if you’re after an nREPL server for some other language that runs on a single thread, the supervisor and the mailbox might give you a few ideas. The server itself knows nothing about threads or sockets, and the trick with the mailbox works for any interpreter that can call back now and then while it runs. What’s next The obvious next step is getting cross-origin isolation on nrepl.org. A service worker can add the headers GitHub Pages won’t send, at the cost of a reload when it’s first installed, so I might try that on a page of its own. I’d also like to allow some harmless interop, so that (.toUpperCase \"hi\") works as people expect, and to publish nrepl-sci on npm. Epilogue Back in 2018 I wrote that there was “the big dream to port nREPL to ClojureScript”, so it could be used in self-hosted environments, and in 2023 I was still hoping we’d see more native ClojureScript nREPL servers. nrepl-sci isn’t quite that dream - it’s a much smaller nREPL server for SCI - but it’s a step in that direction. Big thanks to Michiel Borkent for SCI, which did most of the hard work here. None of this would exist without it! And, as usual, special thanks to Clojurists Together for supporting my work on nREPL! That’s all I have for you today. Play with the REPL, try to break it, and let me know how it went! Keep hacking! CheerpJ’s free tier also covers only non-commercial open-source projects that load it from Leaning Technologies’ CDN. That would have been fine for nrepl.org, but it’s one more string attached. ↩"}, :excerpts [{:text "... lookup took just a few hundred lines of ClojureScript. The whole thing is about 230KB gzipped and it’s...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... people never think about - interrupt and stdin. JavaScript gives you one thread, and SCI evaluates code in...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-11-01T12:43:23.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-11-01T12:43:23.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://jmglov.net/blog/2026-11-01-scittlecastr.html", :title "Scittlecastr: The Scittling", :link "https://jmglov.net/blog/2026-11-01-scittlecastr.html", :published-at #inst "2026-11-01T23:59:59.000-00:00", :content "This post was entirely written by a human, with no assistance from LLMs or other \"AI\" technology such as pervert glasses or autonomous weapon systems. I&aposve mentioned cljcastr here before, but in a very tongue in cheek fashion. Since then, cljcastr has turned into a serious project to create a set of tools for producing and self-hosting podcasts. The project has two simple principles: No surveillance. Ever. cljcastr runs on your computer and only your computer. It does not connect to any external services, store data on the cloud (AKA other people&aposs computers), or track you in any way, shape, or form. No AI. All cljcastr code is written by hand, by humans. Nothing in this repository has been AI generated, and no AI generated pull requests will even be considered for inclusion. cljcastr is hosted on Codeberg at jmglov/cljcastr. In this blog post, I&aposm going to demonstrate the cljcastr ethos by implementing a simple podcast player in ClojureScript, using the mighty Scittle. Building the skeleton To get started, we&aposre going to need a Babashka project set up to serve files from a local webserver. Let&aposs create a cljcastr directory: mkdir cljcastr\n And now pop a bb.edn into it: {:deps {io.github.babashka/sci.nrepl\n        {:git/sha \"80ec956af5e6610b040089ba62eecb5d223e9e76\"}\n        io.github.babashka/http-server\n        {:git/sha \"b38c1f16ad2c618adae2c3b102a5520c261a7dd3\"}}\n\n :tasks\n {http-server {:doc \"Starts http server for serving static files\"\n               :requires ([babashka.http-server :as http])\n               :task (do (http/serve {:port 1341 :dir \"resources/public\"})\n                         (println \"Serving static assets at http://localhost:1341\"))}\n\n  browser-nrepl {:doc \"Start browser nREPL\"\n                 :requires ([sci.nrepl.browser-server :as bp])\n                 :task (bp/start! {})}\n\n  -dev {:depends [browser-nrepl http-server]}\n\n  dev {:doc \"Development build. Starts webserver and watches for changes.\"\n       :task (do (run &apos-dev {:parallel true})\n                 (deref (promise)))}}\n}\n Now create a resources/public directory: mkdir -p resources/public/\n Drop an index.html in that directory: <html xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">\n\n<head>\n  <title>cljcastr episode player</title>\n\n  <script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/scittle@0.8.33/dist/scittle.js\" type=\"application/javascript\"></script>\n  <script>var SCITTLE_NREPL_WEBSOCKET_PORT = 1340;</script>\n  <script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/scittle@0.8.33/dist/scittle.nrepl.js\" type=\"application/javascript\"></script>\n\n  <script type=\"application/x-scittle\" src=\"player.cljs\"></script>\n</head>\n\n<body>\n</body>\n\n</html>\n And finally, a player.cljs in the same directory that does naught but declare a namespace: (ns cljcastr.player)\n\n(js/console.log \"Ready to play!\")\n Thus equipped, invoke Babashka from the top-level cljcastr directory thusly: bb dev\n Babashka will helpfully tell you where you can find your player: Serving static assets at http://localhost:1341\nnREPL server started on port 1339...\nWebsocket server started on 1340...\n If you browse to localhost:1341, you&aposll see an amazingly blank page titled \"cljcastr episode player\". If you open the JavaScript console in dev tools (Ctrl + Shift + i in many browsers), you should see the player reporting for duty:   Ready to play!                                                    core.cljs:80\n> \n So let&aposs play! Interactive development In addition to the HTTP server serving up static files from our resources/public directory, we have an nREPL server listening on port 1339 and a websocket server on port 1340. When we loaded the index.html webpage in the browser, scittle.nrepl opened a websocket connection to port 1340 and started listening for commands. In order to send it some commands, we will need to connect an nREPL client to the nREPL server on port 1339. Let&aposs do it! This section assumes that you are a reasonable person and are thus using Emacs with CIDER, but if you&aposve unreasonably chosen to use a lesser editor or one of those new-fangled \"IDEs\" like Cursive or Calva, you probably know how to operate nREPL in that editor, so just translate accordingly. First, we open up player.cljs in our editor. Next, we use CIDER&aposs cider-connect-cljs command (C-c M-C) to connect to localhost on port 1339, choosing REPL type nbb when prompted. CIDER should open a REPL buffer that says something like this: ;; Connected to nREPL server - nrepl://localhost:1339\n;; CIDER 1.12.0 (Split)\n;;\n;; ClojureScript REPL type: nbb\n;;\nnil> \n Now we&aposre all set up for interactive development, so let&aposs interact. First, execute cider-load-buffer (C-c C-k) in the player.cljs buffer. If you&aposre watching the JS console in your browser, you&aposll see something like the following pop up:   Ready to play!                                                   nrepl.cljs:10\n> \n And meanwhile in your terminal, you&aposll see the nREPL server log some output: :msg \"{:value \\\"nil\\\", :id \\\"246\\\", :session \\\"e443955d-e285-4d5b-bbde-9b7f21f1561c\\\", :ns \\\"cljcastr.player\\\"}\"\n:msg \"{:status [\\\"done\\\"], :id \\\"246\\\", :session \\\"e443955d-e285-4d5b-bbde-9b7f21f1561c\\\", :ns \\\"cljcastr.player\\\"}\"\n We&aposve now completed the circle of LISP, meaning we can control everything on our player page with ClojureScript! 💥 Let&aposs try some simple arithmetic just to get a sense of the power at our fingertips. In the player.cljs buffer, we&aposll add a Rich comment and some code: (comment\n\n  (+ 1 2)\n\n  )\n Positioning our cursor right after the (+ 1 2) form, we hit C-c C-v f c e to execute cider-pprint-eval-last-sexp-to-comment, and are rewarded by a nice comment being inserted on the next line: (comment\n\n  (+ 1 2)\n  ;; => 3\n\n  )\n We can also print this result to the browser console by adding another line inside the (comment) form: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (js/console.log (+ 1 2))\n\n  )\n If you&aposre using CIDER with an autocomplete setup, you should actually see completion candidates popping up as you type. 🤯 If you position the cursor after the form and evaluate it again with C-c C-v f c e, you&aposll get a comment on the next line saying   ;; => nil\n This is somewhat underwhelming, but if you take a peek at your browser console, you&aposll be more satisfied:   3                                                                nrepl.cljs:10\n> \n Can we have our cake and eat it too? (Note that from here on, I&aposll assume you&aposre whacking C-c C-v f c e after the new form you add, which will produce the ;; => comment on the next line.) (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (doto (+ 1 2)\n    js/console.log)\n  ;; => 3\n\n  )\n Now we&aposre cooking with biofuel! Let&aposs try something a little more complex: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (doto (map inc (range 5))\n    js/console.log)\n  ;; => (1 2 3 4 5)\n\n  )\n This is great, but what in the world is happening over in the browser console?   ‣ $APP.gg {J: null, na: null, I: null, F: 32374988, ma: ƒ, …}    nrepl.cljs:10\n> \n If we click on the ‣, things get even wilder:   ‣ $APP.gg {J: null, na: null, I: null, F: 32374988, ma: ƒ, …}    nrepl.cljs:10\n      F: 32374988\n      I: null\n      J: null\n      M: 1\n      ma: null\n    ‣ na: $APP.lg {hc: kg, Zc: $…P.gg, J: null, I: null, F: 31850732, …}\n    ‣ [[Prototype]]: Object\n>\n What we&aposre seeing is a JavaScript representation of a ClojureScript vector. This is of course super cool and all, but not so readable (unless you&aposre Rich Hickey and you don&apost even see the persistent data structures but just the woman in the red dress). Let&aposs see if we can do better: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (doto (->> (map inc (range 5))\n             clj->js)\n    js/console.log)\n  ;; => #js [1 2 3 4 5]\n\n  )\n The browser console is looking good!   ‣ (5) [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]                                            nrepl.cljs:10\n But life inside our (comment) form is less idyllic. What&aposs up with this nonsense?   ;; => #js [1 2 3 4 5]\n Now we&aposre seeing ClojureScript&aposs JavaScript data literal. 😅 Let&aposs try one more thing: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (let [res (map inc (range 5))]\n    (js/console.log (clj->js res))\n    res)\n  ;; => (1 2 3 4 5)\n\n  )\n That&aposs much better! Now how about we extract this to a function so we can use it to log stuff? (ns cljcastr.player)\n\n(defn log [v]\n  (js/console.log (clj->js v))\n  v)\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (log (map inc (range 5)))\n  ;; => (1 2 3 4 5)\n\n  )\n Good stuff. Thus equipped, we can start sprucing up this empty webpage. Getting down to brass HTML Let&aposs add an h1 HTML element to the page to let users know what&aposs up. The obvious way to do this would be to open up index.html and drop in something like this: <body>\n  <h1>cljcastr player</h1>\n</body>\n However, this would require a page reload for us to see the changes, and that would blow away the state we&aposve carefully built up in our REPL. 😭 Let&aposs see if we can do this in the REPL instead. The basic idea is this: Create an h1 element. Set its inner text to \"cljcastr player\". Grab the body element that we helpfully put in our HTML page skeleton. Add the new h1 element as a child of the body element. And if we were to call an imaginary eng->clj function, that would look something like this: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  ;; 1. Create an **h1** element.\n  (def h1 (js/document.createElement \"h1\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/h1\n\n  ;; 2. Set its inner text to \"cljcastr player\".\n  (set! (.-textContent h1) \"cljcastr player\")\n  ;; => \"cljcastr player\"\n\n  ;; 3. Grab the **body** element that we helpfully put in our HTML page skeleton.\n  (def body (js/document.querySelector \"body\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/body\n\n  ;; 4. Add the new **h1** element as a child of the **body** element.\n  (.appendChild body h1)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLHeadingElement [object HTMLHeadingElement]]\n\n  )\n Et voilà! Our boring page has been transformed into an amazing feast for the eyes! Before moving on, let&aposs take a brief detour into a new JavaScript literal:   ;; => #object[HTMLHeadingElement [object HTMLHeadingElement]]\n This is a bit opaque, but we can always feed it to our log function to make it nicely inspectable in the console: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n  \n  (log h1)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLHeadingElement [object HTMLHeadingElement]]\n\n  )\n The console will render this as:   <h1>cljcastr player</h1>                                         nrepl.cljs:10\n Play that funky music Let&aposs find ourselves the URL of a random podcast episode and add an audio element to the page: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def audio (js/document.createElement \"audio\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/audio\n\n  (set! (.-controls audio) true)\n  ;; => true\n\n  (set! (.-src audio)\n        \"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/5d9048546bacd9001de88f1c/2abc6f33-6a0a-4658-a8e0-c811984c13a0.mp3\")\n\n  (->> audio\n       (.appendChild body))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLAudioElement [object HTMLAudioElement]]\n\n  (log audio)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLAudioElement [object HTMLAudioElement]]\n\n)\n Now we should see an audio player pop up on screen! And if we click play, we hear the defn theme music. We could declare victory right here, but we&aposre missing a few things that a podcast player usually has, such as: Sweet custom controls instead of the ugly HTML5 default audio player. The ability to play any podcast episode, not just one we&aposve helpfully downloaded ahead of time. If a transcript is available, display it with clickable timestamps. Let&aposs see what we can do about that. Escaping from vanilla The standard HTML audio control is functional, but a bit boring. In our hubris, we decide we can do way better than that! Let&aposs look at a typical podcast player for inspiration: It looks like we&aposll want the following buttons: Play Pause Skip backward Skip forward Playback speed Let&aposs see if we can find some nice vector graphics for our buttons. SVG Repo is a great place to start, and a search there for \"play\" turns up a nice looking SVG named Play Circle Fill SVG, which is provided under the MIT Licence. That looks promising! Better yet, the SVG is part of a collection called [Framework7 Filled Icons] (https://www.svgrepo.com/collection/framework7-filled-icons/). Let&aposs click into that and see if we can find some SVGs for other useful media player buttons. Searching within the collection, we find a nice Pause button, but nothing for the other three we need. 😭 No matter! We widen our search to all of SVG Repo and find nice graphics for the rest. Here&aposs where we land: Play Pause Skip forward (from Iconsax Bold Oval Icons) Skip backward (from Iconsax Bold Oval Icons) Playback speed (from Awesome Set) Let&aposs create a set of HTML images for each. We&aposll start by defining a map to describe them: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def playback-controls\n    {:play {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/436852/play-circle-fill.svg\"\n            :label \"Play\"}\n     :pause {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/436829/pause-circle-fill.svg\"\n             :label \"Pause\"}\n     :forward {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495321/forward-15-seconds.svg\"\n               :label \"Forward {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"}\n     :backward {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495036/backward-15-seconds.svg\"\n             :label \"Back {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"}\n     :speed {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/151785/speed-meter.svg\"\n             :label \"Playback speed\"}})\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-controls\n\n  (def seek-dur-sec 15)\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/seek-dur-sec\n\n  )\n Now we can map over the map (yeah yeah, I know) to create our img elements: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def playback-buttons\n    (->> playback-controls\n         (map (fn [[control-name {:keys [src label]}]]\n                (let [el (js/document.createElement \"img\")]\n                  (set! (.-id el) (str \"button-\" (name control-name)))\n                  (set! (.-classList el) \"cljcastr playback-icon\")\n                  (set! (.-src el) src)\n                  (set! (.-draggable el) false)\n                  el)))\n         log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-buttons\n\n  )\n Note that we&aposre adding a playback-icon class to the element in addition to our standard cljcastr class, and also giving the element an ID so it&aposs easy to grab it with a CSS selector later. We also set the draggable attribute to false to prevent drag-and-drop silliness from happening. Now, how about a container to hold them? (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def playback-controls-container\n    (-> (let [el (js/document.createElement \"div\")]\n          (set! (.-id el) \"playback-controls\")\n          (set! (.-classList el) \"cljcastr\")\n          el)\n        log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-controls-container\n\n  (doseq [el playback-buttons]\n    (.appendChild playback-controls-container el))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n Finally, let&aposs display the controls! (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (.appendChild body playback-controls-container)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  )\n Let&aposs set the width of the images to a more reasonable size: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (doseq [el playback-buttons]\n    (set! (.-style el) \"width: 35px\"))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n That&aposs much better! However, this black and white page is starting to bum us out. Rainbows and unicorns Since our controls are SVGs, we can use the magic of CSS filters to colour them. Let&aposs start by picking a colour, and what better colour for the sake of this demo than a lovely amber sort of shade called #f7931e. Of course, CSS filters can&apost be specified in something as prosaic as hex colour codes, so we&aposll use a magic CodePen thingy to generate a filter from our hex code. (We could also port the JavaScript from this spectacular StackOverflow answer to ClojureScript, but we kinda don&apost feel like it.) What we come up with is this: filter: invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%) hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%);\n We can now add this as a style to our img tags: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (doseq [el playback-buttons]\n    (set! (.-style el)\n          (str \"width: 35px; filter: invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%) \"\n               \"hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%);\")))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n Better! One thing that&aposs slightly annoying is that we had to remember to include to include the width style when we added the filters. Why don&apost we write some code that adds a style to an existing element whilst preserving existing styles? First, let&aposs help ourselves out by pulling in clojure.string: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  ;; What do styles look like?\n  (-> playback-buttons\n      first\n      (.getAttribute \"style\"))\n  ;; => \"width: 35px; filter: invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%)\n  ;;     hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%);\"\n\n  ;; Parse into a list of styles\n  (-> playback-buttons\n      first\n      (.getAttribute \"style\")\n      (str/split #\";\\s+\"))\n  ;; => [\"width: 35px\"\n  ;;     \"filter: invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%)\n  ;;      hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%);\"]\n\n  ;; Turn it into a map\n  (let [styles (-> playback-buttons\n                   first\n                   (.getAttribute \"style\")\n                   (str/split #\";\\s+\"))]\n    (->> styles\n         (map #(let [[k v] (str/split % #\":\\s+\")]\n                 [(keyword k) v]))\n         (into {})))\n  ;; => {:width \"35px\",\n  ;;     :filter \"invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%)\n  ;;              hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%);\"}\n\n  )\n Now we have a thing that we can easily turn into a function! (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; (defn log ...)\n\n(defn get-styles\n  \"Returns the inline styles of an element as a map of style name to value.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [styles (-> el\n                   (.getAttribute \"style\")\n                   (str/split #\";\\s+\"))]\n    (->> styles\n         (map #(let [[k v] (str/split % #\":\\s+\")]\n                 [(keyword k) v]))\n         (into {}))))\n\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> playback-buttons\n      first\n      get-styles)\n  ;; => {:width \"35px\",\n  ;;     :filter \"invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%)\n  ;;              hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%);\"}\n\n  )\n How about going the other direction, i.e. turning a map of styles into a style string? (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> {:width \"35px\", :height \"35px\"}\n       (map (fn [[k v]]\n              (str (name k) \": \" v)))\n       (str/join \"; \"))\n  ;; => \"width: 35px; height: 35px\"\n\n  )\n From this, &apostis merely a hop, skip, and a jump to write a function that sets the styles of an element: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; (defn get-styles ...)\n\n(defn set-styles!\n  \"Sets the styles of an element from a map of style name to value.\"\n  [el styles]\n  (let [styles-str (->> styles\n                        (map (fn [[k v]]\n                               (str (name k) \": \" v)))\n                        (str/join \"; \"))]\n    (set! (.-style el) styles-str))\n  el)\n\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  ;; Back to black!\n  (doseq [el playback-buttons]\n    (set-styles! el {:width \"35px\"}))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n Finally, let&aposs put this together and create a function that adds a style instead of replacing all the existing ones: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn set-styles! ...)\n\n(defn add-style!\n  \"Adds a style to an element\"\n  [el style-name style-value]\n  (let [styles (get-styles el)]\n    (set-styles! el (assoc styles (name style-name) style-value)))\n  el)\n\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  ;; Amber is the new black\n  (doseq [el playback-buttons]\n    (add-style! el :filter\n                (str \"invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%) \"\n                     \"hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%)\")))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n Play it again, Sam Now that we have visually stunning buttons, let&aposs actually make them do something. Or rather, let&aposs do something that allows us to make them do something. 😅 Remember how we added a cljcastr class to the buttons so we could easier grab them with js/document.querySelector? Well, we didn&apost do this to our poor audio element, which will now cause us a problem: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (js/document.querySelector \"audio\")\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLAudioElement [object HTMLAudioElement]]\n\n  )\n Seems OK in the REPL, but if we look at the JS console, things (might not, depending on your browser and which extensions you have installed) look so good:   <audio class=\"audio-for-speech\" src></audio>                     nrepl.cljs:10\n Seems like my browser is set up with a screen reader, which is very nice for accessibility, but not nice for Clojure programmers who didn&apost get enough hammock time. Let&aposs add a class so we can use a more precise selector: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (set! (.-classList audio) \"cljcastr\")\n  ;; => \"cljcastr\"\n\n  (-> (js/document.querySelector \"audio.cljcastr\")\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLAudioElement [object HTMLAudioElement]]\n\n  )\n And now the console looks more like what we expected:   <audio class=\"cljcastr\" controls                                 nrepl.cljs:10\n  src=\"https://media.zencastr.com/audio-files/5d9048546bacd9001de88f1c/2abc6f33-6a0a-4658-a8e0-c811984c13a0.mp3\">\n  </audio>\n Now we can proceed with making our buttons come alive: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  ;; Grab the play button\n  (def play-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-play\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/play-button\n\n  ;; Add a click handler\n  (.addEventListener play-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (.play (js/document.querySelector \"audio.cljcastr\"))))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n If we click the button, we hear the podcast start playing, and can see the playback position in the standard HTML audio element above. Now we can do the same for the pause button: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  ;; Grab the pause button\n  (def pause-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-pause\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/play-button\n\n  ;; Add a click handler\n  (.addEventListener pause-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (.pause (js/document.querySelector \"audio.cljcastr\"))))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n That was easy! Skipping forward will be a little more interesting, since there&aposs no JS function to tell the audio object \"skip forward x seconds\", there&aposs only a currentTime property that we can query and set: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (.-currentTime audio)\n  ;; => 29.396358\n\n  (set! (.-currentTime audio) 15.0)\n  ;; => 15\n\n  )\n Given this, it&aposs not too hard to figure out how to skip forward and backward an arbitrary number of seconds: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  ;; Forward\n  (let [cur (.-currentTime audio)]\n    (set! (.-currentTime audio) (+ cur seek-dur-sec)))\n  ;; => 30\n\n  ;; Backward\n  (let [cur (.-currentTime audio)]\n    (set! (.-currentTime audio) (- cur seek-dur-sec)))\n  ;; => 15\n\n  )\n It&aposs probably a good idea not to skip forward past the end of the episode or backward past the beginning, so let&aposs see if we can put a stop to that silliness: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  ;; Forward with guard\n  (let [cur (.-currentTime audio)]\n    (set! (.-currentTime audio) (min (+ cur seek-dur-sec)\n                                     (.-duration audio))))\n  ;; => 30\n\n  ;; The end is nigh!\n  (set! (.-currentTime audio) (- (.-duration audio) 5))\n  ;; => 5365.949958\n\n  ;; Don&apost worry, all is well\n  (let [cur (.-currentTime audio)]\n    (set! (.-currentTime audio) (min (+ cur seek-dur-sec)\n                                     (.-duration audio))))\n  ;; => 5370.949958\n\n  ;; Hitting 88 miles per hour\n  (set! (.-currentTime audio) 5)\n  ;; => 5\n\n  ;; Backward\n  (let [cur (.-currentTime audio)]\n    (set! (.-currentTime audio) (max (- cur seek-dur-sec)\n                                     0)))\n  ;; => 0\n\n  )\n Armed with this code, let&aposs make some functions: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn add-style! ...)\n\n(defn skip-forward! [audio-el sec]\n  (let [cur (.-currentTime audio-el)]\n    (set! (.-currentTime audio-el) (min (+ cur sec)\n                                        (.-duration audio-el)))))\n\n(defn skip-backward! [audio-el sec]\n  (let [cur (.-currentTime audio-el)]\n    (set! (.-currentTime audio-el) (max (- cur sec)\n                                        0))))\n\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (skip-forward! audio seek-dur-sec)\n  ;; => 15\n\n  (skip-backward! audio seek-dur-sec)\n  ;; => 0\n\n  )\n Now we can wire up the skip buttons as well: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def forward-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-forward\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/forward-button\n\n  (def backward-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-backward\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/backward-button\n\n  (.addEventListener forward-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (skip-forward! audio seek-dur-sec)))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  (.addEventListener backward-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (skip-backward! audio seek-dur-sec)))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n Look at this! All of our buttons work! (Except the playback speed one, but that&aposs a little more complicated, so let&aposs save it for later. 😉) The only thing that&aposs obviously wrong with this amazing player is that it has two sets of controls, the standard HTML one and our amazing buttons. There&aposs also something that&aposs not obviously wrong to most readers, unless they happen to be using a screen reader: the accessibility of our buttons sucks! In fact, the only way we know they&aposre buttons is by their appearance, because we&aposre using img elements, which are semantically just images. They could be images of an elephant for all a screen reader knows. Let&aposs see what we can do about that! A11y spells ally From the excellent MDN article on Accessibility: Accessibility (often abbreviated to A11y — as in, \"a\", then 11 characters, and then \"y\") in web development means enabling as many people as possible to use websites, even when those people&aposs abilities are limited in some way. For many people, technology makes things easier. For people with disabilities, technology makes things possible. Accessibility means developing content to be as accessible as possible, no matter an individual&aposs physical and cognitive abilities and how they access the web. The W3C Accessibility Initiative puts it in even more inspiring terms: The Web is fundamentally designed to work for all people, whatever their hardware, software, language, location, or ability. When the Web meets this goal, it is accessible to people with a diverse range of hearing, movement, sight, and cognitive ability. We certainly want all people to adopt our superior podcast player, so let&aposs make sure it works for them, using the power of ARIA! No, not that one. 😉 This one: ARIA. If we wrap each of our img elements in a button element, we&aposre now conveying semantic meaning to a screen reader (or any other user agent, for that matter; imagine a script that is trying to identify all the buttons on a page... such as the one we&aposre in the process of writing). And if we set that button&aposs aria-label attribute, we&aposre now identifying what the button is meant to do. Remember the :label key we included in our playback-controls map but never used way back when? (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def playback-controls\n    {:play {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/436852/play-circle-fill.svg\"\n            :label \"Play\"}\n     :pause {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/436829/pause-circle-fill.svg\"\n             :label \"Pause\"}\n     :forward {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495321/forward-15-seconds.svg\"\n               :label \"Forward {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"}\n     :backward {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495036/backward-15-seconds.svg\"\n             :label \"Back {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"}\n     :speed {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/151785/speed-meter.svg\"\n             :label \"Playback speed\"}})\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-controls\n\n  )\n Turns out we had accessibility in mind all along! Let&aposs try wrapping a button: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (let [button-name :play\n        {:keys [src label]} {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495321/forward-15-seconds.svg\"\n                             :label \"Forward {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"}]\n    (let [button-id (str \"button-\" (name button-name))\n          button-el (js/document.createElement \"button\")\n          img-el (js/document.createElement \"img\")]\n      (set! (.-id button-el) button-id)\n      (set! (.-classList button-el) \"cljcastr\")\n      (set! (.-ariaLabel button-el) label)\n      (set! (.-id img-el) (str button-id \"-icon\"))\n      (set! (.-classList img-el) \"cljcastr playback-icon\")\n      (set! (.-src img-el) src)\n      (set! (.-draggable img-el) false)\n      (.appendChild button-el img-el)\n      (log button-el)))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n\n  )\n All of this set! stuff is starting to get annoying. Why don&apost we write a nice function that we can call to give us back a fully baked DOM element? (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn add-style! ...)\n\n(defn create-el [{:keys [tag attrs] :as args}]\n  (let [el (js/document.createElement tag)]\n    (doseq [[attr v] attrs]\n      (.setAttribute el (name attr) v))\n    el))\n\n;; (defn skip-forward! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (let [button-name :play\n        {:keys [src label]} {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495321/forward-15-seconds.svg\"\n                             :label \"Forward {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"}]\n    (let [button-id (str \"button-\" (name button-name))\n          button-el (create-el {:tag \"button\"\n                                :attrs {:id button-id\n                                        :class \"cljcastr\"\n                                        :aria-label label}})\n          img-el (create-el {:tag \"img\"\n                             :attrs {:id (str button-id \"-icon\")\n                                     :class \"cljcastr playback-icon\"\n                                     :src src\n                                     :draggable false}})]\n      (.appendChild button-el img-el)\n      (log button-el)))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n\n  )\n This looks pretty reasonable in the JS console:   ‣ <button class=\"cljcastr\" id=\"button-play\"                      nrepl.cljs:10\n            aria-label=\"Forward {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\">\n      <img class=\"cljcastr playback-icon\" id=\"button-play-icon\"\n           src=\"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495321/forward-15-seconds.svg\"\n           draggable=\"false\">\n    </button>\n Except this bit, that is: aria-label=\"Forward {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"\n Looks like we got a bit clever there with some Selmer-style templating to allow for changing the number of seconds we&aposre skipping around. Let&aposs quickly figure out how to interpolate such strings: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (let [s \"Something {{something}} something\"]\n    (str/replace s \"{{something}}\" \"SOMETHING\"))\n  ;; => \"Something SOMETHING something\"\n\n  (let [s \"Something {{something}} something\"\n        ctx {:something \"SOMETHING\"}]\n    (str/replace s #\"\\{\\{(something)\\}\\}\" (fn [[_ k]] (get ctx (keyword k)))))\n  ;; => \"Something SOMETHING something\"\n\n  )\n This may appear slightly inscrutable at first glance, but if we look at the documentation for clojure.string/replace, we see that the replacement argument can actually be a function, in which case it&aposs passed the match as an argument and whatever it returns is the replacement string. When the match contains a capturing group, the argument to the function is a vector of [match group1 group2 ...]. For example: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (let [s \"It&aposs easy as 123, ABC, baby you and me\"]\n    (str/replace s #\"(\\d)+.+([A-Z]+)\"\n                 (fn [match-vector] (str \"<<<\" (pr-str match-vector) \">>>\"))))\n  ;; => \"It&aposs easy as <<<[\\\"123, ABC\\\" \\\"3\\\" \\\"C\\\"]>>>, baby you and me\"\n\n  )\n Let&aposs turn this into a proper function: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; (defn log ...)\n\n(defn render\n  \"Given a string `s` and a context map `ctx`, replaces template variables of the\n   form {{k}} with the value of the corresponding key in the context map. If\n   the key `k` doesn&apost exist in the context map, the template variable won&apost be\n   replaced (i.e. \\\"{{foo}}\\\" will remain \\\"{{foo}}\\\" in the interpolated\n   string).\"\n  [s ctx]\n  (str/replace s #\"\\{\\{([^}]+)\\}\\}\"\n               (fn [[match k]] (get ctx (keyword k) match))))\n\n;; (defn get-styles ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (render \"foo {{bar}} baz {{blah}} {{barf}}\" {:bar 27, :blah 42})\n  ;; => \"foo 27 baz 42 {{barf}}\"\n\n  )\n Now we can make use of our new render function in our button-wrapping code: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (let [button-name :play\n        {:keys [src label]} {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495321/forward-15-seconds.svg\"\n                             :label \"Forward {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"}]\n    (let [button-id (str \"button-\" (name button-name))\n          button-el (create-el {:tag \"button\"\n                                :attrs {:id button-id\n                                        :class \"cljcastr\"\n                                        :aria-label (render label {:seek-dur-sec seek-dur-sec})}})\n          img-el (create-el {:tag \"img\"\n                             :attrs {:id (str button-id \"-icon\")\n                                     :class \"cljcastr playback-icon\"\n                                     :src src\n                                     :draggable false}})]\n      (.appendChild button-el img-el)\n      (log button-el)))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n\n  )\n Quoth the console:   ‣ <button class=\"cljcastr\" id=\"button-forward\"                      nrepl.cljs:10\n            aria-label=\"Forward 15 seconds\">\n      <img class=\"cljcastr playback-icon\" id=\"button-forward-icon\"\n           src=\"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495321/forward-15-seconds.svg\"\n           draggable=\"false\">\n    </button>\n Now we can extract a nice function to create a button: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn create-el ...)\n\n(defn create-button\n  ([button-name button]\n   (create-button button-name button {}))\n  ([button-name {:keys [label src click-handler]} ctx]\n   (let [button-id (str \"button-\" (name button-name))\n         button-el (create-el {:tag \"button\"\n                               :attrs {:id button-id\n                                       :class (render \"cljcastr {{button-class}}\" ctx)\n                                       :aria-label (render label ctx)}})\n         img-el (create-el {:tag \"img\"\n                            :attrs {:id (str button-id \"-icon\")\n                                    :class (render \"cljcastr {{img-class}}\" ctx)\n                                    :src src\n                                    :draggable false}})]\n     (.addEventListener button-el \"click\" click-handler)\n     (.appendChild button-el img-el)\n     button-el)))\n\n;; (defn skip-forward! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> (create-button :forward\n                      {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495321/forward-15-seconds.svg\"\n                       :label \"Forward {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"}\n                      {:seek-dur-sec seek-dur-sec\n                       :button-class \"playback-button\"\n                       :img-class \"playback-icon\"})\n       log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n\n  )\n We&aposve pulled another nice thing out of Clojure&aposs bag of tricks: multi-arity functions: \n(defn create-button\n  ([button-name button]\n   (create-button button-name button {}))\n  ([button-name {:keys [label src]} ctx]\n   ;; ...\n  ))\n This allows us to call create-button without a context: (create-button :play {:label \"Play\", :src \"play.svg\"})\n This will call the version of create-button that takes two arguments, which in turn calls the three-argument version, passing along the button-name and button arguments as is, and adding an empty map as the ctx argument. OK, now we can use our create-button function to sew new a11y&aposd buttons onto our playback-controls div: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  ;; Throw away the current crappy buttons\n  (.replaceChildren playback-controls-container)\n  ;; => nil\n\n  ;; Let&aposs fix the order of our buttons whilst we&aposre at it\n  (def playback-buttons\n    (->> [:backward :play :pause :forward :speed]\n         (map (fn [button-name]\n                [button-name (get playback-controls button-name)]))\n         (map (fn [[button-name button]]\n                (let [button-el (create-button button-name button\n                                               {:seek-dur-sec seek-dur-sec\n                                                :button-class \"playback-button\"\n                                                :img-class \"playback-icon\"})]\n                  (-> button-el\n                      .-firstChild\n                      (set-styles! {:width \"35px\"\n                                    :filter \"invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%) hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%)\"}))\n                  button-el)))\n         log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-buttons\n\n  (doseq [el playback-buttons]\n    (.appendChild playback-controls-container el))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  ;; Grab the new buttons\n  (def play-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-play\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/play-button\n\n  (def pause-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-pause\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/play-button\n\n  (def forward-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-forward\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/forward-button\n\n  (def backward-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-backward\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/backward-button\n\n  ;; Attach the click handlers to them\n  (.addEventListener play-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (.play (js/document.querySelector \"audio.cljcastr\"))))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  (.addEventListener pause-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (.pause (js/document.querySelector \"audio.cljcastr\"))))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  (.addEventListener forward-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (skip-forward! (js/document.querySelector \"audio.cljcastr\")\n                                      seek-dur-sec)))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  (.addEventListener backward-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (skip-backward! (js/document.querySelector \"audio.cljcastr\")\n                                       seek-dur-sec)))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n The only problem with this approach is that our buttons now look like, well, buttons. 😅 Let&aposs restyle them a bit so they look like they did before: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn create-el ...)\n\n(defn create-button\n  ([button-name button]\n   (create-button button-name button {}))\n  ([button-name {:keys [label src click-handler]} ctx]\n   (let [button-id (str \"button-\" (name button-name))\n         button-el (create-el {:tag \"button\"\n                               :attrs {:id button-id\n                                       :class (render \"cljcastr {{button-class}}\" ctx)\n                                       :aria-label (render label ctx)}})\n         img-el (create-el {:tag \"img\"\n                            :attrs {:id (str button-id \"-icon\")\n                                    :class (render \"cljcastr {{img-class}}\" ctx)\n                                    :src src\n                                    :draggable false}})]\n     (set-styles! button-el {:padding 0\n                             :margin 0\n                             :border 0\n                             :background \"transparent\"\n                             :cursor \"pointer\"})\n     (.addEventListener button-el \"click\" click-handler)\n     (.appendChild button-el img-el)\n     button-el)))\n\n;; (defn skip-forward! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (.replaceChildren playback-controls-container)\n  ;; => nil\n\n  (def playback-buttons\n    (->> [:backward :play :pause :forward :speed]\n         (map (fn [button-name]\n                [button-name (get playback-controls button-name)]))\n         (map (fn [[button-name button]]\n                (let [button-el (create-button button-name button\n                                               {:seek-dur-sec seek-dur-sec\n                                                :button-class \"playback-button\"\n                                                :img-class \"playback-icon\"})]\n                  (-> button-el\n                      .-firstChild\n                      (set-styles! {:width \"35px\"\n                                    :filter (str \"invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%) \"\n                                                 \"hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%)\")}))\n                  button-el)))\n         log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-buttons\n\n  (doseq [el playback-buttons]\n    (.appendChild playback-controls-container el))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  (def play-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-play\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/play-button\n\n  (def pause-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-pause\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/play-button\n\n  (def forward-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-forward\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/forward-button\n\n  (def backward-button (js/document.querySelector \"#button-backward\"))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/backward-button\n\n  (.addEventListener play-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (.play (js/document.querySelector \"audio.cljcastr\"))))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  (.addEventListener pause-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (.pause (js/document.querySelector \"audio.cljcastr\"))))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  (.addEventListener forward-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (skip-forward! audio seek-dur-sec)))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  (.addEventListener backward-button \"click\"\n                     (fn [ev]\n                       (log ev)\n                       (skip-backward! audio seek-dur-sec)))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n Now our buttons look like they did before! 🎉 However, recreating these buttons is getting a little tedious. Let&aposs do some cleaning up. Sweeping up the shop First of all, our log function is a little limited. It would be nice if we could include a log message in addition to whatever data we&aposre logging. In other words, we&aposd like to do this: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (log \"Something happened!\" {:foo 1, :bar 2})\n  ;; => \"Something happened!\"\n\n  )\n But if we look at the console now, we only see the message logged and not the data:   Something happened!                                                 nrepl.cljs:10\n Luckily, the fix is pretty straightforward: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n(defn log [& args]\n  (apply js/console.log (map clj->js args))\n  (last args))\n\n;; (defn render ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (log \"Something happened!\" {:foo 1, :bar 2})\n  ;; => {:foo 1, :bar 2}\n\n  )\n The value returned is what we wanted, and the console looks good as well:   Something happened!  ‣ {foo: 1, bar: 2}                             nrepl.cljs:10\n Next annoyance: all those js/document.querySelector calls. Extraction! (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn render ...)\n\n(defn get-el\n  \"Returns the element matching the specified CSS selector. If `selector` is\n   already an element, just returns the element.\"\n  [selector]\n  (if (instance? js/Element selector)\n    selector\n    (js/document.querySelector selector)))\n\n;; (defn get-styles ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (get-el \"#button-play\")\n  ;; => #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n\n  )\n Might as well have a function to get a list of elements too: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn get-el ...)\n\n(defn get-els\n  \"Returns a sequence containing the elements matching the specified CSS\n   selector.\"\n  [selector]\n  (->> (js/document.querySelectorAll selector)\n       seq))\n\n;; (defn get-styles ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (get-els \"#playback-controls > button\")\n  ;; => (#object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]] \n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]] \n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]] \n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]] \n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]])\n\n  )\n How about one to return the children of an element? (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn get-els ...)\n\n(defn get-children [el]\n  (-> (get-el el)\n      .-childNodes\n      seq))\n\n;; (defn get-styles ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#playback-controls\")\n      log)\n  ;; => (#object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]] \n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]] \n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]] \n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]] \n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]])\n\n  )\n We added another nicety there: by using get-el, our function can take either an element or a CSS selector as its argument. Let&aposs continue to use this pattern as we add a function for adding a child to an element: But if we look at the console, we see something extremely odd:                                                                       nrepl.cljs:10\n  ‣ he {value: button#backward, Ee: ArrayIterator, Ag: null, F: 8388672, M: 0}\n The answers, my friend, are blowing in the wind of get-children: (defn get-children [el]\n  (-> (get-el el)\n      .-childNodes\n      seq))\n We&aposre taking the childNodes attribute of our HTMLDivElement, which is a NodeList object: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-el \"#playback-controls\")\n      .-childNodes\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[NodeList [object NodeList]]\n\n  )\n This looks great in the console:                                                                       nrepl.cljs:10\n    NodeList(5) [button#button-backward.cljcastr.playback-button,\n    button#button-play.cljcastr.playback-button,\n  ‣ button#button-pause.cljcastr.playback-button,\n    button#button-forward.cljcastr.playback-button,\n    button#button-speed.cljcastr.playback-button]\n But we can&apost use it in Clojure all that well: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-el \"#playback-controls\")\n      .-childNodes\n      count)\n  ;; => \"#error {:message \\\"No protocol method ICounted.-count defined for type object: [object NodeList]\\\", ...}\"\n\n  )\n That&aposs why get-children adds the call to seq at the end. That lets normal Clojure code deal with the list of children, but as we&aposve seen, it doesn&apost look good in the console when logged. Let&aposs see what we can do about this: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#playback-controls\")\n      vec\n      log)\n  ;; => [#object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]]\n\n  )\n Now the console is looking quite reasonable!                                                                       nrepl.cljs:10\n    (5) [button#button-backward.cljcastr.playback-button,\n    button#button-play.cljcastr.playback-button,\n  ‣ button#button-pause.cljcastr.playback-button,\n    button#button-forward.cljcastr.playback-button,\n    button#button-speed.cljcastr.playback-button]\n Let&aposs see if we can apply what we&aposve learned here to our log function: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n(defn log [& args]\n  (let [seq->vec #(if (seq? %) (vec %) %)]\n    (apply js/console.log (map (comp clj->js seq->vec) args)))\n  (last args))\n\n  ;; (defn render ...)\n  ;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> (get-children \"#playback-controls\")\n      log)\n  ;; => (#object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n  ;;     #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]])\n\n  )\n (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn get-children ...)\n\n(defn add-child! [parent-el el]\n  (let [parent-el (get-el parent-el)]\n    (-> parent-el\n        (.appendChild el))\n    parent-el))\n\n;; (defn get-styles ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> (add-child! \"#playback-controls\"\n                   (create-el {:tag \"span\"\n                               :text \"Some nonsense\"}))\n       (log \"I got a new child\"))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  )\n Wanna clear all the children of an element? (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn add-child! ...)\n\n(defn clear-children! [el]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (.replaceChildren el)\n    el))\n\n;; (defn get-styles ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> (clear-children! \"#playback-controls\")\n       (log \"Empty nest\"))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  )\n Set the children of an element? (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn clear-children! ...)\n\n(defn set-children! [parent-el els]\n  (let [parent-el (get-el parent-el)]\n    (clear-children! parent-el)\n    (doseq [el els]\n      (add-child! parent-el el))\n    parent-el))\n\n;; (defn get-styles ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (->> (set-children! \"#playback-controls\" playback-buttons)\n       log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  )\n Final annoyance: all the repetition in the click handlers. Let&aposs extract a function for that as well: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn create-el ...)\n\n(defn add-event-listener! [el event-type f]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (.addEventListener el event-type\n                       (fn [ev]\n                         (log (str \"Handling \" event-type \" on\")\n                              el ev)\n                         (if f\n                           (f ev)\n                           (log \"No event handler function defined\"))))\n    (log \"Added listener for\" event-type \"events to\" el)\n    el))\n\n;; (defn create-button ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (add-event-listener! \"#button-speed\" \"click\"\n                       #(log \"Should probably do something with this event, eh?\" %))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLButtonElement [object HTMLButtonElement]]\n\n  )\n And if we now click the playback speed button, the console rewards us with some rich logging: With all these awesome functions, we can finally recreate our buttons much more concisely: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn add-event-listener! ...)\n\n(defn create-button\n  ([button-name button]\n   (create-button button-name button {}))\n  ([button-name {:keys [label src click-handler]} ctx]\n   (let [button-id (str \"button-\" (name button-name))]\n     (-> (create-el {:tag \"button\"\n                     :attrs {:id button-id\n                             :class (render \"cljcastr {{button-class}}\" ctx)\n                             :aria-label (render label ctx)}})\n         (set-styles! {:padding 0\n                       :margin 0\n                       :border 0\n                       :background \"transparent\"\n                       :cursor \"pointer\"})\n         (add-event-listener! \"click\" click-handler)\n         (add-child! (create-el {:tag \"img\"\n                                 :attrs {:id (str button-id \"-icon\")\n                                         :class (render \"cljcastr {{img-class}}\" ctx)\n                                         :src src\n                                         :draggable false}}))))))\n\n;; (defn skip-forward! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def playback-controls\n    {:play {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/436852/play-circle-fill.svg\"\n            :label \"Play\"\n            :click-handler #(.play (get-el \"audio.cljcastr\"))}\n     :pause {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/436829/pause-circle-fill.svg\"\n             :label \"Pause\"\n             :click-handler #(.pause (get-el \"audio.cljcastr\"))}\n     :forward {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495321/forward-15-seconds.svg\"\n               :label \"Forward {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"\n               :click-handler #(skip-forward! (get-el \"audio.cljcastr\") seek-dur-sec)}\n     :backward {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/495036/backward-15-seconds.svg\"\n                :label \"Back {{seek-dur-sec}} seconds\"\n                :click-handler #(skip-backward! (get-el \"audio.cljcastr\") seek-dur-sec)}\n     :speed {:src \"https://www.svgrepo.com/show/151785/speed-meter.svg\"\n             :label \"Playback speed\"}})\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-controls\n\n  (def playback-buttons\n    (->> [:backward :play :pause :forward :speed]\n         (map (fn [button-name]\n                [button-name (get playback-controls button-name)]))\n         (map (fn [[button-name button]]\n                (let [button-el (create-button button-name button\n                                               {:seek-dur-sec seek-dur-sec\n                                                :class \"playback-control\"})]\n                  (-> (get-children button-el)\n                      first\n                      (set-styles! {:width \"35px\"\n                                    :filter \"invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%) hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%)\"}))\n                  button-el)))\n         log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-buttons\n\n  (set-children! \"#playback-controls\" playback-buttons)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  )\n OK, so we&aposve fixed the non-obvious (to those of us not using screen readers) problem with the player. Scratching our heads, we try to recall what the obvious problem was. 🤔 Oh yeah! Two sets of controls, right. Let&aposs fix. Sweeping audio under the carpet Making our audio element disappear isn&apost that hard. Let&aposs use this as an excuse to create a few more helper functions and spread the get-el pattern a little wider as well: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn get-els ...)\n\n(defn get-attr\n  \"Returns the value of the specified attribute.\"\n  [el attr]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (.getAttribute el (name attr))))\n\n(defn set-attr!\n  \"Sets the value of the specified attribute.\"\n  [el attr v]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (.setAttribute el (name attr) v)\n    el))\n\n(defn set-attrs! [el attrs]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (doseq [[k v] attrs]\n      (.setAttribute el (name k) v))\n    el))\n\n(defn get-id\n  \"Returns the ID of an element.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (.-id el)))\n\n(defn set-id!\n  \"Sets the ID of an element.\"\n  [el id]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (when-not (empty? id)\n      (set-attr! el :id id))\n    el))\n\n(defn get-name\n  \"Returns the tag name of an element.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (.-localName el)))\n\n(defn get-text\n  \"Returns the inner text of an element.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (or (.-textContent el)\n        (.-innerText el))))\n\n(defn set-text! [el text]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (when-not (empty? text)\n      (set! (.-textContent el) text))\n    el))\n\n(defn get-classes\n  \"Returns the classes of the specified element as a list of class names.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (->> (str/split (.-classList el) #\"\\s\")\n         (remove empty?))))\n\n(defn set-classes!\n  \"Sets the classes of the specified element. Classes may either be a list or a\n   string.\n\n   Example:\n\n     ;; These two are equivalent\n     (set-classes el [\\\"foo\\\" \\\"bar\\\"])\n     (set-classes el \\\"foo bar\\\")\"\n  [el classes]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (when-not (empty? classes)\n      (set! (.-classList el) (if (string? classes)\n                               classes\n                               (str/join \" \" classes))))\n    el))\n\n;; (defn get-children ...)\n;; ...\n;; (defn set-children! ...)\n\n(defn get-styles\n  \"Returns the inline styles of an element as a map of style name to value.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [styles (-> (get-el el)\n                   (.getAttribute \"style\")\n                   (str/split #\";\\s+\"))]\n    (->> styles\n         (remove empty?)\n         (map #(let [[k v] (str/split % #\":\\s+\")]\n                 [(keyword k) v]))\n         (into {}))))\n\n(defn set-styles!\n  \"Sets the styles of an element from a map of style name to value.\"\n  [el styles]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (when-not (empty? styles)\n      (set! (.-style el) (->> styles\n                              (map (fn [[k v]]\n                                     (str (name k) \": \" v)))\n                              (str/join \"; \"))))\n    el))\n\n(defn add-styles!\n  \"Given a map of style name to value, adds the styles to the element.\"\n  [el styles]\n  (-> el\n      (set-styles! (merge (get-styles el) styles))))\n\n(defn add-style!\n  \"Adds a style to an element\"\n  [el style-name style-value]\n  (-> el\n      (add-styles! {style-name style-value})))\n\n;; (defn create-el)\n;; ...\n Now that we have all of this awesomeness, we can streamline create-el by accepting a few more keys: :id \"foo\" instead of :attrs {:id \"foo\"} :classes [\"foo\" \"bar\"] instead of :attrs {:class \"foo bar\"} :text to set the text content of the element We also have to update create-button to take advantage of this. (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn add-style! ...)\n\n(defn create-el [{:keys [tag id classes text attrs] :as args}]\n  (-> (js/document.createElement tag)\n      (set-attrs! attrs)\n      (set-id! id)\n      (set-classes! classes)\n      (set-text! text)))\n\n;; (defn add-event-listener! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(defn create-button\n  ([button-name button]\n   (create-button button-name button {}))\n  ([button-name {:keys [label src click-handler]} ctx]\n   (let [button-id (str \"button-\" (name button-name))]\n     (-> (create-el {:tag \"button\"\n                     :id button-id\n                     :classes [\"cljcastr\"\n                               (render \"{{button-class}}\" ctx)]\n                     :attrs {:aria-label (render label ctx)}})\n         (set-styles! {:padding 0\n                       :margin 0\n                       :border 0\n                       :background \"transparent\"\n                       :cursor \"pointer\"})\n         (add-event-listener! \"click\" click-handler)\n         (add-child! (create-el {:tag \"img\"\n                                 :id (str button-id \"-icon\")\n                                 :classes [\"cljcastr\"\n                                           (render \"{{img-class}}\" ctx)]\n                                 :attrs {:src src\n                                         :draggable false}}))))))\n\n;; (defn skip-forward! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (add-style! audio :display \"none\")\n  ;; => #object[HTMLAudioElement [object HTMLAudioElement]]\n\n  (def playback-buttons\n    (->> [:backward :play :pause :forward :speed]\n         (map (fn [button-name]\n                [button-name (get playback-controls button-name)]))\n         (map (fn [[button-name button]]\n                (let [button-el (create-button button-name button\n                                               {:seek-dur-sec seek-dur-sec\n                                                :button-class \"playback-button\"\n                                                :img-class \"playback-icon\"})]\n                  (-> (get-children button-el)\n                      first\n                      (set-styles! {:width \"35px\"\n                                    :filter (str \"invert(59%) sepia(92%) saturate(611%) \"\n                                                 \"hue-rotate(343deg) brightness(97%) contrast(99%)\")}))\n                  button-el)))\n         log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-buttons\n\n  (set-children! \"#playback-controls\" playback-buttons)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  )\n Testing our controls, we verify that they still work. However, we&aposve lost three important things: The progress bar, which gave us a visual indication of where we are and a way to move to an arbitrary point in the episode by clicking or dragging. The display of the current position in the episode. The display of the duration of the episode. Actually, on the third point, we like Overcast&aposs choice to display the time remaining instead of the duration, so let&aposs do that as well. Having made these bold design decisions, let&aposs get to implementing! We&aposll start with the easy stuff, displaying the time elapsed and remaining: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def elapsed-time (create-el {:tag \"span\"\n                                :text (str (.-currentTime audio))\n                                :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                :id \"elapsed-time\"}))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/cur-time\n\n  (def remaining-time (create-el {:tag \"span\"\n                                  :text (str (- (.-duration audio)\n                                                (.-currentTime audio)))\n                                  :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                  :id \"remaining-time\"}))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/remaining-time\n\n  (doseq [el [elapsed-time remaining-time]]\n    (add-child! \"#playback-controls\" el))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n Well, this is something, at least. Something incredibly confusing and also ugly. 😭 Let&aposs enumerate the problems: Times are displayed as fractional seconds, not the more familiar h:mm:ss. The spans are side by side with no padding, so it&aposs not possible to tell where elapsed time ends and remaining time begins. The font size is too large compared to the buttons. And maybe black isn&apost the best choice of colour anyway. We&aposd like the times displayed above the buttons (and the progress bar, when we get around to adding it). Now that we know what&aposs wrong, let&aposs tackle things one at a time. Hours, minutes, and seconds, oh my! How can we convert from seconds to h:mm:ss? (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (set! (.-currentTime audio) 3645)\n  ;; => 3645\n\n  (let [secs (.-currentTime audio)\n        h (-> secs (/ 3600) int)\n        mm (-> secs (/ 60) (- (* h 60)) int)\n        ss (-> secs (mod 60) int)]\n    (str (int h) \":\" (int mm) \":\" (int ss)))\n  ;; => \"1:0:45\"\n\n  )\n OK, not a bad start. Let&aposs zero-pad the minutes and seconds, though, because \"1:00:45\" looks way better than \"1:0:45\". 😉 (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (let [secs (.-currentTime audio)\n        h (-> secs (/ 3600) int)\n        mm (-> secs (/ 60) (- (* h 60)) int)\n        ss (-> secs (mod 60) int)\n        zero-pad #(-> % int str (str/replace #\"^(\\d)$\" \"0$1\"))]\n    (str (int h) \":\" (zero-pad mm) \":\" (zero-pad ss)))\n  ;; => \"1:00:45\"\n\n  (set! (.-currentTime audio) 46)\n  ;; => 46\n\n  (let [secs (.-currentTime audio)\n        h (-> secs (/ 3600) int)\n        mm (-> secs (/ 60) (- (* h 60)) int)\n        ss (-> secs (mod 60) int)\n        zero-pad #(-> % int str (str/replace #\"^(\\d)$\" \"0$1\"))]\n    (str (int h) \":\" (zero-pad mm) \":\" (zero-pad ss)))\n  ;; => \"0:00:46\"\n\n  )\n Hrm, maybe one more tweak. It&aposs conventional to omit the hour(s) portion of timestamps less than an hour: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (let [secs (.-currentTime audio)\n        h (-> secs (/ 3600) int)\n        mm (-> secs (/ 60) (- (* h 60)) int)\n        ss (-> secs (mod 60) int)\n        zero-pad #(-> % int str (str/replace #\"^(\\d)$\" \"0$1\"))]\n    (str (if (pos? (int h))\n           (str (int h) \":\")\n           \"\")\n         (zero-pad mm) \":\" (zero-pad ss)))\n  ;; => \"00:46\"\n\n  )\n Nice! Now let&aposs turn this into a function: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn render ...)\n\n(defn secs->ts\n  \"Given a number of seconds, returns a timestamp of the form h:mm:ss.\"\n  [secs]\n  (let [h (-> secs (/ 3600) int)\n        mm (-> secs (/ 60) (- (* h 60)) int)\n        ss (-> secs (mod 60) int)\n        zero-pad #(-> % int str (str/replace #\"^(\\d)$\" \"0$1\"))]\n    (str (if (pos? (int h))\n           (str (int h) \":\")\n           \"\")\n         (zero-pad mm) \":\" (zero-pad ss))))\n\n;; (defn get-el ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  ;; Elapsed time\n  (secs->ts (.-currentTime audio))\n  ;; => \"00:46\"\n\n  ;; Remaining time\n  (secs->ts (- (.-duration audio) (.-currentTime audio)))\n  ;; => \"1:28:44\"\n\n  (-> elapsed-time\n      (set-text! (secs->ts (.-currentTime audio)))\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLSpanElement [object HTMLSpanElement]]\n\n  (-> remaining-time\n      (set-text! (str \"-\" (secs->ts (- (.-duration audio) (.-currentTime audio)))))\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLSpanElement [object HTMLSpanElement]]\n  \n  )\n OK, we&aposve solved problem number 1. 🎉 Problems 2 and 3 are just styling, so the fixes are pretty straightforward: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (doseq [el [elapsed-time remaining-time]]\n    (add-styles! el {:margin \"0 5px 0 5px\"\n                     :font-size \"0.7rem\"\n                     :color \"grey\"}))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n Whilst we&aposre at it, let&aposs fix another annoyance: with our current create-el function, we either have to call add-styles! (or set-styles!) afterwards, or do the stringifying of the styles ourselves: (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n            :attrs {:id \"whatever\"\n                    :style \"margin: 0 5px 0 5px; font-size: 0.7rm; color: grey\"}})\n We also have the same issue with classes and ID. 🙁 But instead of wallowing in misery, let&aposs modify create-el to take a :styles key: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn add-style! ...)\n\n(defn create-el [{:keys [tag id classes styles text attrs] :as args}]\n  (-> (js/document.createElement tag)\n      (set-attrs! attrs)\n      (set-id! id)\n      (set-classes! classes)\n      (set-styles! styles)\n      (set-text! text)))\n\n;; (defn add-event-listener! ...)\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (let [el (-> (create-el {:tag \"span\"\n                           :id \"elapsed-time\"\n                           :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                           :text (secs->ts 123)\n                           :styles {:margin \"0 5px 0 5px\"\n                                    :font-size \"0.7rem\"\n                                    :color \"grey\"}})\n               log)]\n    (add-child! \"#playback-controls\" el))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  )\n Now, let&aposs address the last problem. Laying out the law It is now time to employ the most awesome technology known to humankind to figure out our layout: ASCII art! +-----------------------------------+\n|            Podcast title          |\n+-----------------------------------+\n|            Episode title          |\n+-----------------------------------+\n|            Release date           |\n+-----------------------------------+\n|            Episode art            |\n+-----------------------------------+\n|     Elapsed time | Remaining time |\n+-----------------------------------+\n|            Progress bar           |\n+-----------------------------------+\n| Backward | Play | Pause | Forward |\n+-----------------------------------+\n|           Playback speed          |\n+-----------------------------------+\n We&aposve been using the body element to hold our player, but we should probably deploy a div at this point so we can style things according to our liking. We can also group similar things together in divs, something like this: + episode-container ----------------------------+\n|                                               |\n| + episode-intro ----------------------------+ |\n| |                Podcast title              | |\n| +-------------------------------------------+ |\n| |                Episode title              | |\n| +-------------------------------------------+ |\n| |                Release date               | |\n| +-------------------------------------------+ |\n|                                               |\n+-----------------------------------------------+\n|                  Episode art                  |\n+-----------------------------------------------+\n|                Audio (hidden)                 |\n+-----------------------------------------------+\n|                                               |\n| + progress ---------------------------------+ |\n| |       Elapsed time | Remaining time       | |\n| +-------------------------------------------+ |\n| |                Progress bar               | |\n| +-------------------------------------------+ |\n|                                               |\n+-----------------------------------------------+\n|                                               |\n| + playback-controls ------------------------+ |\n| | Backward | Play | Pause | Forward | Speed | |\n| +-------------------------------------------+ |\n|                                               |\n+-----------------------------------------------+\n Let&aposs start by creating the new nested containers (we already have one for playback controls) and populating them with some hard-coded data: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def episode-container (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                                     :id \"episode-container\"\n                                     :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                     :styles {:width \"100%\"\n                                              :display \"flex\"\n                                              :flex-direction \"column\"\n                                              :align-items \"center\"}}))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/episode-container\n\n  (def episode-info\n    (-> (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                    :id \"episode-info\"\n                    :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                    :styles {:max-width \"420px\"\n                             :text-align \"center\"}})\n        (set-children! [(create-el {:tag \"h3\"\n                                    :id \"podcast-title\"\n                                    :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                    :text \"defn\"})\n                        (create-el {:tag \"h2\"\n                                    :id \"episode-title\"\n                                    :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                    :text \"876b4c306172 Cora Sutton\"})\n                        (create-el {:tag \"span\"\n                                    :id \"episode-date\"\n                                    :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                    :styles {:color \"grey\"}\n                                    :text \"July 9, 2025\"})])\n        log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/episode-info\n\n  (def episode-art\n    (-> (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                    :id \"episode-art-container\"\n                    :classes [\"cljcastr\"]})\n        (add-child! (create-el {:tag \"img\"\n                                :id \"episode-art\"\n                                :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                                :styles {:width \"400px\"\n                                         :border-radius \"5%\"\n                                         :padding \"10px\"}\n                                :attrs {:src \"https://media.zencastr.com/image-files/5d9048546bacd9001de88f1c/a5b922ac-7294-4d58-9a33-4308f5d4f50b.png\"}}))\n        log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/episode-art\n\n  (def progress (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                            :id \"progress\"\n                            :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                            :styles {:width \"390px\"\n                                     :margin \"1.5em auto 0 auto\"}}))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/progress\n\n  (def playback-controls-container (-> (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                                                   :id \"playback-controls\"\n                                                   :classes [\"cljcastr\"]})\n                                       (set-children! playback-buttons)\n                                       log))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-controls-container\n\n  (def playback-speed (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                                  :id \"playback-speed\"\n                                  :classes [\"cljcastr\"]}))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/playback-speed\n\n  (-> episode-container\n      (set-children! [episode-info\n                      episode-art\n                      (-> audio\n                          (set-styles! {:display \"none\"}))\n                      progress\n                      playback-controls-container\n                      playback-speed])\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  (set-children! \"body\" [episode-container])\n  ;; => #object[HTMLBodyElement [object HTMLBodyElement]]\n\n  )\n This is looking really good (relatively speaking), but there&aposs one major component missing. Slipping and sliding We have no idea where in the episode we are! Let&aposs see about getting that progress bar in place. Luckily, HTML has got us covered! There just so happens to be a progress element designed exactly for what we want to do! (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def progress-bar\n    (create-el {:tag \"progress\"\n                :id \"progress-bar\"\n                :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                :attrs {:min 0\n                        :max (.-duration audio)\n                        :value (.-currentTime audio)}\n                :styles {:appearance \"none\"\n                         :height \"8px\"\n                         :width \"100%\"\n                         :border \"0\"\n                         :border-radius \"4px\"\n                         :margin \"0\"\n                         :overflow \"hidden\"\n                         :background-color \"lightgray\"\n                         :color \"#f7931e\"}}))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/progress-bar\n\n  )\n Now that we have the progress bar, let&aposs add it to our #progress div, along with the current and elapsed time spans we created before: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (-> progress\n      (set-children! [(-> elapsed-time\n                          (add-styles! {:float \"left\"}))\n                      (-> remaining-time\n                          (add-styles! {:float \"right\"}))\n                      progress-bar])\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  )\n Hurrah! I mean, it&aposs a bit ugly, but at least it&aposs there! 😅 Let&aposs click the play button, then skip forward a few times. Now, we can try setting the value of the progress bar to the current time of the audio element: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (set! (.-value progress-bar) (.-currentTime audio))\n  ;; => 370.277385\n\n  )\n We should see the progress bar move along a bit. This is great and all, but it would be lovely to have a way to actually move forward and backward in the episode by clicking or dragging on the progress bar. You know, the way every audio player ever works? Sadly, the progress bar itself isn&apost really designed for this. We could certainly hack it up with some \"click\" event listeners and a lot of arithmetic, but surely there must be a better way, right? And indeed there is! HTML has an input element of type range that is designed for exactly this case! Let&aposs try it out: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def progress-slider\n    (create-el {:tag \"input\"\n                :id \"progress-slider\"\n                :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                :attrs {:type \"range\"\n                        :min 0\n                        :max (.-duration audio)\n                        :value (.-currentTime audio)\n                        :step \"any\"\n                        :aria-label \"Seek\"\n                        :aria-valuetext (str (secs->ts (.-currentTime audio))\n                                             \" of \"\n                                             (secs->ts (.-duration audio)))\n                        :aria-valuemax (.-duration audio)}\n                :styles {:appearance \"none\"\n                         :cursor \"pointer\"\n                         :height \"28px\"\n                         :width \"100%\"\n                         :border \"0\"\n                         :margin \"0\"\n                         :touch-action \"manipulation\"}}))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/progress-slider\n\n  (-> progress\n      (set-children! [(-> elapsed-time\n                          (add-styles! {:float \"left\"}))\n                      (-> remaining-time\n                          (add-styles! {:float \"right\"}))\n                      progress-slider\n                      progress-bar])\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  )\n OK, a blue dot has appeared. Let&aposs skip forward in the episode a bit more, then update the value of the slider to the current playback position. Oh yeah, might as well update the value of the progress bar as well, whilst we&aposre at it: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (doseq [el [progress-slider progress-bar]]\n    (set! (.-value el) (.-currentTime audio)))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n Now, how about setting the playback position in the episode when we click or drag the slider? (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (add-event-listener! progress-slider \"input\"\n                       #(->> % .-target .-value (set! (.-currentTime audio))))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLInputElement [object HTMLInputElement]]\n\n  )\n We now have a pretty good representation of playback position: A span showing the elapsed time A span showing the remaining time A slider letting us control the playback position A progress bar giving us a visual indication of the playback position Let&aposs see if we can keep all of these in sync. First, some code to update them all in one go: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (let [secs (.-currentTime audio)\n        duration-secs (.-duration audio)]\n    (set-text! elapsed-time (secs->ts secs))\n    (set-text! remaining-time (str \"-\" (secs->ts (- duration-secs secs))))\n    (.setAttribute progress-slider \"aria-valuetext\"\n                   (str (secs->ts secs) \" of \" (secs->ts duration-secs)))\n    (doseq [el [progress-slider progress-bar]]\n      (set! (.-value el) (.-currentTime audio))))\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n Looks like that works, so let&aposs extract it into a function. And whilst we&aposre at it, maybe we can extract functions for the attribute and value setting so we don&apost have to expose the raw DOM interactions. And for the sake of completeness, we can add getters as well: (ns cljcastr.player\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n;; ...\n;; (defn set-text! ...)\n\n(defn get-value\n  \"Returns the value attribute of the specified element.\"\n  [el]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (.-value el)))\n\n(defn set-value!\n  \"Sets the value attribute of the specified element.\"\n  [el v]\n  (let [el (get-el el)]\n    (set! (.-value el) v)))\n\n;; (defn set-classes! ...)\n;; ...\n;; (defn skip-backward! ...)\n\n(defn update-playback-position-indicators!\n  \"Given a current playback time and duration (both in seconds) and a map of\n   elements, updates the elements to reflect the current playback position. The\n   element map should contain the following keys:\n   `:elapsed-time` - text element (for example: label, span, etc.)\n   `:remaining-time` - text element (for example: label, span, etc.)\n   `:progress-slider` - input element of type range\n   `:progress-bar` - progress element\"\n  [current-secs duration-secs\n   {:keys [elapsed-time remaining-time progress-slider progress-bar]}]\n  (set-text! elapsed-time (secs->ts current-secs))\n  (set-text! remaining-time (str \"-\" (secs->ts (- duration-secs current-secs))))\n  (set-attr! progress-slider :aria-valuetext\n             (str (secs->ts current-secs) \" of \" (secs->ts duration-secs)))\n  (doseq [el [progress-slider progress-bar]]\n    (set-value! el current-secs)))\n\n;; ...\n\n(comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (update-playback-position-indicators! (.-currentTime audio) (.-duration audio)\n                                        {:elapsed-time elapsed-time\n                                         :remaining-time remaining-time\n                                         :progress-slider progress-slider\n                                         :progress-bar progress-bar})\n  ;; => nil\n\n  )\n There&aposs one final touch we should apply here before we move on. It&aposs getting a bit silly to have to manually update these elements, right? How about we update them automatically as the episode plays? For this, we can use the timeupdatechange event of our audio element: (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (add-event-listener! audio \"timeupdate\"\n                       #(update-playback-position-indicators!\n                         (.-currentTime audio) (.-duration audio)\n                         {:elapsed-time elapsed-time\n                          :remaining-time remaining-time\n                          :progress-slider progress-slider\n                          :progress-bar progress-bar}))\n  ;; => #object[HTMLAudioElement [object HTMLAudioElement]]\n\n  )\n Now things are running smoothly, but there&aposs another thing that&aposs bothering us. The progress slider that we use to control the playback position is above the progress bar, which just looks weird, and also goes against the expectations of our hordes of users that there should be a handle on the progress bar itself that they can drag around. You know, like this: Getting into position What we&aposd really like is to set our slider down right on top of the progress bar. This will give us the seeking we seek (sorry) as well as a visual indication of the current playback position. In order to do this, we&aposre going to have to learn a bit about CSS positioning. Basically, we&aposll need to create a new container to hold the progress bar and the slider, and set its position style to \"relative\". Then, we can add our progress bar and slider as children of it, setting their position to \"absolute\" (relative to their parent container). Yes, I know, this is enough to make our heads explode, but I guess it makes sense once you achieve enlightenment or something? We also need to set the z-indexes of the two elements. We set the progress bar to 0 to put it at the bottom of the stack, and the slider to 1 so it&aposs on top. That means we need to make the background colour of the slider transparent so the progress bar can shine through in all its glory. Finally, we position both elements 0 pixels from the left of the enclosing container, then the slider 0 pixels from the top and the progress bar 10 pixels from the top. Why 10 pixels? Well, because: The slider is 28 pixels high. The progress bar is 8 pixels high. We want to vertically centre the progress bar underneath the slider. 20 - 8 is 10. With that advanced mathematics out of the way, let&aposs make it happen! (comment\n\n  ;; ...\n\n  (def progress-container\n    (create-el {:tag \"div\"\n                :id \"progress-container\"\n                :classes [\"cljcastr\"]\n                :styles {:position \"relative\"\n                         :height \"28px\"\n                         :clear \"both\"}}))\n  ;; => #&aposcljcastr.player/progress-container\n\n  (-> progress-container\n      (set-children! [(-> progress-bar\n                          (add-styles! {:position \"absolute\"\n                                        :top \"10px\"\n                                        :left \"0px\"\n                                        :z-index 0}))\n                      (-> progress-slider\n                          (add-styles! {:position \"absolute\"\n                                        :top \"0px\"\n                                        :left \"0px\"\n                                        :z-index 1\n                                        :background-color \"transparent\"}))])\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  (-> progress\n      (add-styles! {:position \"relative\"})\n      (set-children! [(-> elapsed-time\n                          (add-styles! {:float \"left\"}))\n                      (-> remaining-time\n                          (add-styles! {:float \"right\"}))\n                      progress-container])\n      log)\n  ;; => #object[HTMLDivElement [object HTMLDivElement]]\n\n  )\n This is looking pretty good! There are a few more things we might want to do, but just as we&aposre about to start doing some of those things, a bone-chilling thought occurs to us: what if we reload the page? We&aposve been building our player up in the REPL one s-expression at a time, but our HTML file still has a body that looks like this: <body>\n</body>\n Should a feline friend prance across the keyboard, stepping on the Ctrl and r keys simultaneously (or Apple-r, if you&aposre one of those too-cool-for-school types), all will be lost! Before giving in to despair, we realise that we have the awesome power of ClojureScript at our fingertips, and thus can make a permanent record of what we&aposve done so far. And that, my dear readers, we shall endeavour to do in the new post in this series, which shall be called Scittlecastr: All You Need is Persistence."}, :excerpts [{:text "... by implementing a simple podcast player in ClojureScript, using the mighty Scittle. Building the skeleton...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... type=\"application/javascript\"></script>\n  <script>var...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... type=\"application/javascript\"></script>\n\n  <script...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]}]